{"id":"reorganize-string","title":"Reorganize String","difficulty":"Medium","pattern":"Heap / Priority Queue","tags":["Hash Table","String","Greedy","Heap","Counting"],"companies":{"google":2,"apple":2,"meta":3,"amazon":4,"microsoft":2},"description":"Given a string `s`, rearrange its characters so that **no two adjacent characters are the same**.\n\nReturn any valid rearrangement, or the empty string `\"\"` if none exists.","examples":[{"input":"s = \"aab\"","output":"\"aba\""},{"input":"s = \"aaab\"","output":"\"\"","explanation":"Three a's need at least two separators between them, but there is only one b."},{"input":"s = \"aabb\"","output":"\"abab\"","explanation":"\"baba\" is also accepted."}],"constraints":["1 <= s.length <= 500","s consists of lowercase English letters."],"hints":["When is it impossible? Think about how many slots the most common character needs.","If any character appears more than ceil(len(s) / 2) times, return \"\". Otherwise a greedy works: always place the most frequent remaining character that differs from the last one placed.","Use a max-heap of (count, char). Pop the top, append it, and hold it back one round before re-inserting it so it cannot be placed twice in a row."],"function":"reorganizeString","signature":"def reorganizeString(self, s: str) -> str:","arg_types":{"s":"str"},"return_type":"str","compare":"any_of","tests":[{"input":{"s":"aab"},"output":["aba"]},{"input":{"s":"aaab"},"output":[""]},{"input":{"s":"aabb"},"output":["abab","baba"]},{"input":{"s":"a"},"output":["a"]},{"input":{"s":"ab"},"output":["ab","ba"]},{"input":{"s":"aaabb"},"output":["ababa"]},{"input":{"s":"aaaabb"},"output":[""]},{"input":{"s":"aaabc"},"output":["abaca","acaba"]},{"input":{"s":"vvvlo"},"output":["vlvov","vovlv"]},{"input":{"s":""},"output":[""]},{"input":{"s":""},"output":[""]},{"input":{"s":""},"output":["",""]}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def reorganizeString(self, s: str) -> str:\n        counts = Counter(s)\n        if max(counts.values()) > (len(s) + 1) // 2:\n            return \"\"\n\n        heap = [(-count, ch) for ch, count in counts.items()]   # max-heap by count\n        heapify(heap)\n        result = []\n        held = None   # the character placed last round, kept out of the heap for one turn\n\n        while heap:\n            count, ch = heappop(heap)\n            result.append(ch)\n            if held is not None:\n                heappush(heap, held)\n            held = (count + 1, ch) if count + 1 < 0 else None\n\n        return \"\".join(result)\n","approach":"**Feasibility check, then greedy by frequency with a one-round hold-back.** A character with c copies needs c - 1 gaps, so it fits only if c <= ceil(len(s) / 2). If the most frequent character passes this test, a valid arrangement always exists and the greedy below finds one.\n\n**Algorithm:** max-heap of `(-count, char)`. Each round pop the most frequent character, append it, then push back the character held from the *previous* round (it has now been separated by one position), and hold the current one if it still has copies left. Because the just-used character is never in the heap at the next pop, no two adjacent characters are equal.\n\n**Why it never gets stuck:** suppose the heap becomes empty while a character is still held with copies remaining. That means every other character ran out while this one still needed slots, which contradicts the feasibility bound; so with the bound checked up front the loop always consumes everything. Equivalently, the greedy keeps the gap between the most frequent letter's copies filled with the next most frequent letters, which is the best possible use of separators.\n\n**Alternative without a heap:** sort characters by frequency and place them into the even indices 0, 2, 4, ... first, then the odd indices. O(n) after counting, and a nice follow-up to offer.\n\n**Common mistakes:** pushing the used character straight back (it may be popped again immediately); popping two characters per round and forgetting the case where only one is left; off-by-one in the feasibility bound (`(len + 1) // 2`, not `len // 2`); returning a partially built string instead of \"\" when the input is impossible.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** require a distance of at least k between equal characters (same idea with a queue of k held items, rearrange-string-k-distance-apart); return the lexicographically smallest valid arrangement; count the number of valid arrangements; the even/odd slot method and why it is correct.","complexity":{"time":"O(n log 26) = O(n)","space":"O(26) for the heap plus O(n) for the output"},"similar":["task-scheduler","rearrange-string-k-distance-apart","sort-characters-by-frequency"],"number":86,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def reorganizeString(self, s: str) -> str:\n        pass\n"}